          "title": "You'll want to know: nachgeben",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nachgeben — \"to give in, to give way\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Am Ende hat meine Schwester nachgegeben, und wir haben den Film gesehen. In the end my sister gave in, and we watched the film."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Separable and strong: sie gibt nach, sie hat nachgegeben. Also of things: Der Boden gibt nach — the ground gives way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nachgeben is built from nach \"after\" and geben \"to give\", inherited from Old High German geban and Proto-Germanic gebaną; EtymDB glosses the compound \"to give way, to stretch\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
